A||||10||SCCA FIERO||Removing a broken bolt?||||1||
Z||000000||SCCA FIERO||03-17-2000||06:27 PM||||The bolt that holds the brake caliper on is broken off inside the strut. How can I get this thing out? It's below the surface and there is no way of grabbing it. I have heard on an easy-out but never seen one. Do these things work? If not what other options do I have?<P>I'm talking about the rear caliper on an 88.<p>[This message has been edited by SCCA FIERO (edited 03-17-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Patrick||03-17-2000||07:18 PM||||<BR>Hey Chris, here's the scoop on an easy-out.<P>There are at least two types that I'm aware of. Without getting into a long description of them, suffice to say that they have a tapered shape and come in different sizes. One type hammers in, the other screws in (with a reverse thread). Of course, you're now wondering what the heck it is that they're supposed to be screwed or hammered into. Well, you have to drill a hole into the broken bolt. Drill right through the other end if you can. Make the hole almost as big as the bolt <I>without</I> removing any material from whatever it is that the bolt is stuck in. Insert the easy-out using the method depending on its type, put a wrench on the easy-out, and remove the offending bolt.<P><B>*** ONE WORD OF ADVICE ***</B><P>Do not, I repeat, do not put too much strain on the easy-out when you are trying to unscrew the broken bolt. If the easy-out snaps off in there, you'll have one heck of a time trying to get it out. Trying to drill a hardened steel easy-out without damaging the softer metal around it is not my idea of a good time... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||william_thorniii||03-17-2000||07:21 PM||||SCCA,<P>Usually when I used to break a bolt, I would spend hours and hours trying to extract it.  In the mean time I would try easy outs, heat, welding and any other way I could think of.  Most of the time I would end up just tired and poorer (from breaking the easy-outs etc.).  Anymore when faced with a broken bolt... I take it to a local shop and let them deal with it.  It ends up less expensive and MUCH less frustrating.  Of course if you want, by all means tear into it that bolt would be one of the easier ones to get out (easy to get to, visible and non-hardened).  Easy outs basiclly work like a reverse thread tap.  <P>I guess that didn't help much,<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||13
Z||000003||SCCA FIERO||03-17-2000||07:44 PM||||Thanks Patrick and William 3<P>I have an extra part that the bolt is broken off in. I was hoping to use the one thats on my car now so I won't have to get an alignment too. I guess I will try to get it out, but if it doesn't work... no biggie.||||reg||1
Z||000004||RFMike||03-17-2000||07:46 PM||||Drill out for the extractor.  Then soak it down with WD40 for at least a couple of hours.  Then, thread the extractor in.  Now, heat the metal around the broken bolt with a torch.  Tapping the wrench you have on the bolt may loosen it better than straight pressure.<BR>I concur- if you break of the extractor in the bolt, you will be one unhappy camper.  Experience speaking.<BR>Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000005||ROB MAC||03-17-2000||07:59 PM||||Use a centre punch to mark the bolt before you drill and start with a smaller drill bit and work up in case it goes ooff center . Cutting fluid is handy to avoid breaking the bits and I find Liquid Wrench works better than WD40 to loosen bolts. Don't forget to retap the threads.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Oreif||03-18-2000||01:03 AM|||| Another trick to use is drill the hole thru the bolt and tap in a cheap allen wrench. Then soak in liquid wrench heat metal and it should come out. I will never use an easy-out again. But if you want to try one, I have found a way to get a broken easy-out removed without burning up numerous drill bits. I broke a sway bar bolt and then snapped off an easy-out in it. After many questions/answers and advice from many shops, The best way to remove it is get yourself a Dremel tool and buy the "RED" grinding stones. They come in different diameters. Get one that is the size of the easy-out and start grinding! It took me 4 bits to get thru mine and about 15 minutes of work. If you are very careful and go slow you won't damage the threads. The mechanic who showed me how to do this demonstrated it on an engine head that had a broken exhaust manifold bolt with an easy out stuck in it.  ||||reg||14
Z||000007||SCCA FIERO||03-18-2000||01:35 AM||||Right now I have a hole drilled all the way through the bolt. I hammered an easy-out in it and tried to turn it. The easy-out twisted! I sprayed it good with WD-40 and gonna let it sit over night and try again tomorrow.||||reg||1
Z||000008||SCCA FIERO||03-18-2000||07:13 PM||||It's out  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000009||Raydar||03-18-2000||07:43 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Patrick:<BR><B><P>[b]*** ONE WORD OF ADVICE ***</B><P>Do not, I repeat, do not put too much strain on the easy-out when you are trying to unscrew the broken bolt. If the easy-out snaps off in there, you'll have one heck of a time trying to get it out. Trying to drill a hardened steel easy-out without damaging the softer metal around it is not my idea of a good time...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> [/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Been there. Done that. (Was a starter bolt. In a Ford.)<BR>Ended up calling a tow truck. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000010||SCCA FIERO||03-18-2000||09:48 PM||||Ummm.... that didn't happen to me. Honest... I swear... what's with all the questions? OK, OK... I broke one off. Are you happy now? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||12
